Subject: [PATCH 02/10] D-51 (R4.2, M-PHYSICS-023): J2 in the point-mass ascent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The second half of the owner's "ทำพร้อมกัน" (docs/DECISIONS.md D-51): the
point-mass model's RK4 flies gravityJ2, as the six-DOF body, the orbital
phase and PEG's predictor already did. With the sites on WGS-84 (previous
commit) the field at a pad, with the Earth's turn, is Somigliana's normal
gravity to 5 parts in a million; J2 alone on the old sphere pads made it
worse above ~35° of latitude, which is why the two go together.

- simulation.ts: the point-mass RK4 uses useJ2; the gravity loss is the J2
  field projected on the velocity (-g.v̂ dt), so dvThrust - losses = Δ|v|
  still closes; the E02 record carries gravityJ2 for 'pointMass' and
  'heldCoast', the central field only for the analytic Kepler coast.
- ship-descent.ts: the point-mass ship's descent field is J2.
- debris.ts: separated point-mass hardware flies J2, and its descent
  prediction (descentModel) agrees.
- equations-model.ts: the gravity row checks the point mass against J2;
  the central-field note now speaks of the analytic coasts only (en/ru/th).

Not changed (asked, not answered): the exo-atmospheric Kepler coasts stay
pure conics. IGM keeps its spherical mean gravity.

